  "mechanismSummary": "Fluoride from TBAF adds to the silicon of the TBS ether, giving a five-coordinate fluorosilicate. The alkoxide leaves, and an aqueous ammonium chloride work-up protonates it. The very strong Si–F bond (about 570 kJ mol⁻¹, against about 450 kJ mol⁻¹ for Si–O) drives the reaction. Fluoride removes silyl ethers and leaves almost every other group alone, so a TBS group can be taken off without touching esters, acetals or carbamates. Acid also removes TBS ethers, more slowly than THP or MOM acetals.",
